{-
   **A tree of bitwise operators over a 33-to-53-bit integer, unpacked once instead of once per
   operator.**

   `WideInt` is a host `number` on JS and the operators JS has for one stop at 32 bits, so each of
   `and`, `or`, `xor` and `not` is a call to a helper that splits the value into two 32-bit halves,
   works on each half, and joins them back - see codegen/js/wide.cpp. Composing two of those splits
   and joins twice for no reason: the halves never interact under any of these operators, so the
   whole tree can run on each half separately and be joined once. That is the peephole in opt.cpp,
   measured at 2.2-2.3x on a chain in benchmark/bits53-js.

   **What this fixture asserts is that the fused form computes the same numbers**, which is the only
   assertion worth making about a rewrite whose whole content is that two different expressions are
   equal. The goldens record the shape and the run records the value, and the value is the one that
   would notice a half being masked at the wrong width, a signed high half being read back
   unsigned, or a constant's halves being split the wrong way round. Every case is a distinct power
   of two, so `main` is 255 when they all pass and any other number names the failures in binary.

   Native is unaffected by every line of this - a `WideInt` is an ordinary masked 64-bit integer
   there - which is exactly what makes it the reference the JS run is held to. Confirmed to bite by
   replacing the fused sign extension with the mask the helpers use, which fails exactly the two
   cases whose high half is negative and nothing else.

   The operands are *parameters* rather than literals, and that is a requirement rather than a
   style: fusion declines a tree of nothing but constants, since expanding one would be the same
   arithmetic in more text. WideInt.yana is written the other way round for the same reason, and
   between them the two shapes cover both paths.
-}

fn check(actual: WideInt, expected: WideInt, weight: Int) -> Int =
    if actual == expected then weight else 0

-- ## A mask against a constant, which is what the tree collapses furthest

-- Every bit of the constant is below 32, so the high half of the answer is known to be empty and
-- the whole expression is the low half: one `&` and the unsigned read of it. This is the case the
-- `known` bound in wide.cpp exists for - without it the empty high half is still masked and sign
-- extended, three operations that cannot change anything.
fn andLow(x: WideInt) -> WideInt = x `and` 255

-- The mirror image: the constant is a single bit above 32, so the *low* half is empty and the
-- answer is the high half scaled back up. Nothing is added to it, which is the other end of the
-- same fold.
fn andHigh(x: WideInt) -> WideInt = x `and` 34359738368

-- ## Trees

-- Three operands and two operators over one value, which is the shape the benchmark's `mix3`
-- measures. `a` is split once here and twice as calls.
fn mix(a: WideInt, b: WideInt, c: WideInt) -> WideInt = (a `and` b) `xor` (a `or` c)

-- The read-clear-set-store a flags word actually gets: clear a bit above 32, set one below it, and
-- fold in another word. Four operators, one unpack of each leaf, one join.
--
-- The ascription is not decoration. `not` is a *function*, and an argument of one is a position the
-- expected type does not reach - the literal would settle to `Int`, truncate to zero, and clear
-- nothing. The out-of-range warning is what says so, and it said so about this line.
fn flags(a: WideInt, b: WideInt) -> WideInt =
    ((a `and` not(2199023255552 :: WideInt)) `or` 128) `xor` b

-- ## The sign, which is where the fused form differs from the helpers rather than merely inlining

-- The helpers mask the high half and re-apply the sign afterwards with a ternary that mentions its
-- operand three times, which is why they need a binding for it. Fusing sign-*extends* the high half
-- instead, which is one expression and produces the negative result directly.
fn complement(x: WideInt) -> WideInt = not(x)

-- The same thing reached the other way: a negative operand masked down to a positive one. The
-- constant's low half is all ones, so the `and` on that half is the identity and drops out, and the
-- value has to come back through `>>> 0` rather than as a signed 32-bit result.
fn clearTop(x: WideInt) -> WideInt = x `and` 4503599627370495

-- A chain that starts negative and stays negative, so the sign extension is load-bearing at the
-- join rather than a no-op that happens to be right.
fn keepNegative(x: WideInt) -> WideInt = not(x) `or` 15

-- ## Where fusion declines

fn wholeMask() -> WideInt = 1099511627775

-- A leaf that is a call cannot be split, because splitting reads a leaf once per half and calling
-- something twice is not the same program. So this one stays two helper calls, and the golden is
-- where that shows.
fn andCall(x: WideInt) -> WideInt = x `and` wholeMask()

fn main() -> Int =
    check(andLow(34359738375), 7, 1) +
    check(andHigh(34359738375), 34359738368, 2) +
    check(mix(34359738375, 1099511627775, 255), 248, 4) +
    check(flags(2199023255560, 4398046511109), 4398046511245, 8) +
    check(complement(34359738375), (0 :: WideInt) - 34359738376, 16) +
    check(clearTop((0 :: WideInt) - 1), 4503599627370495, 32) +
    check(keepNegative(1048591), (0 :: WideInt) - 1048577, 64) +
    check(andCall(34359738375), 34359738375, 128)
